WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.737
-Our first guest tonight is
a talented actor you know from

00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:05.304
shows such as
"Little Fires Everywhere"

00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:06.673
and "Grey's Anatomy."

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.141
He recently made his Broadway
debut in "Take Me Out,"

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:10.609
which is currently playing at
the Hayes Theater.

00:00:10.609 --> 00:00:14.080
Please welcome to the show
Jesse Williams, everybody.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.282
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:27.861
How are you?

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:29.495
-I'm amazing.
Thanks for having me.

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:32.264
-Thank you for making time for
us on your on your one day off.

00:00:32.264 --> 00:00:34.367
-Yeah. Yeah.
It's a tight squeeze these days.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.702
-I mean, I would imagine
the routine and the schedule for

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:38.471
a show like "Grey's Anatomy"
is intense,

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:39.839
but this is your Broadway debut.

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:40.973
-Yeah.

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:42.474
-I can't imagine
there's anything quite like

00:00:42.474 --> 00:00:43.543
the Broadway schedule.

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:44.844
-It's insane.
-Yeah.

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:46.178
-It's pretty crazy.

00:00:46.178 --> 00:00:48.148
Now that we're done with
tech and the rehearsals --

00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:50.216
We were doing
10-to-12-hour days of rehearsal,

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.119
then doing a show
eight, nine times a week.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.286
Now we're just doing our show.

00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:56.555
So you get there
a couple hours before.

00:00:56.555 --> 00:00:58.758
But it is a high-wire act.

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.960
It's so much more
physical than TV.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.962
-Yeah. And I think one of
the hardest parts about TV,

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:04.397
which people
don't really understand,

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:07.066
is how it's like sitting around
and waiting for your time,

00:01:07.066 --> 00:01:08.768
whereas especially with
a show like this,

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.703
there's not a lot of
sitting around for you.

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:12.905
-It's so physical and complete.

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.707
I am absolutely
depleted and exhausted

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:16.108
after every single show.

00:01:16.108 --> 00:01:18.444
-And, yet, you also know you
have to do it the very next day.

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:20.980
-Or in two hours,
because we do two a day.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.682
-Yeah, so two a day
twice a week or once week?

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:23.950
-Do it now two a day,
twice a week.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.318
I mean, in previews,
it was a show Friday night,

00:01:26.318 --> 00:01:28.187
two Saturday, two Sunday.

00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:29.555
Then we moved into
two Wednesday.

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:31.757
-So, does it at least
feel like less work now

00:01:31.757 --> 00:01:33.759
after what you went through
in the previews and tech first?

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:34.994
-Yes. It was --
-Okay.

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.796
-The boot camp was
so intense that everything,

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:38.998
yeah, somehow, you're happy.

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:41.967
-And did you feel, like, a lot
of pressure about that?

00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:44.570
Were you stressed out
the opening night?

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:45.738
-I really wasn't.

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:46.773
I feel like I was
supposed to be.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:47.907
-Yeah.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:49.808
-Everybody put a lot of
stress on me --

00:01:49.808 --> 00:01:52.077
not only just for it's
my first time doing a play --

00:01:52.077 --> 00:01:53.612
-Yeah. Ever.
-Yeah.

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:56.749
Doing like a three-act play.
I've done a one-act before once.

00:01:56.749 --> 00:02:00.119
And, so, everybody's --
You know, they're --

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:01.421
You know,
they're so excited about it.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:03.056
Like, "Are you sure?
Are you sure? Are you sure?"

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:04.424
And then there's nudity
in the play,

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:07.292
so, again, like, projecting
all of this fear on to me.

00:02:07.292 --> 00:02:10.129
And I just don't really --
I'm not -- I'm not scared.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:11.363
It was fun.

00:02:11.363 --> 00:02:14.266
-So, this is a play that
covers sexuality and race.

00:02:14.266 --> 00:02:17.103
And, you know, obviously,
it's a baseball play, as well.

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:18.204
And so there's
a locker-room scene,

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:20.072
and you mentioned the nudity.

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:22.842
What were -- What was
your prediction of what

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:25.644
the reaction would be versus
what the reaction actually was?

00:02:25.644 --> 00:02:28.547
-I had -- Something kind of
got planted in my head.

00:02:28.547 --> 00:02:31.117
Me and my a good friend Adepero

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:33.619
went to see "Soldier's Play"
right before --

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:35.722
We were in rehearsals for
this play before COVID happened,

00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:37.089
so two and a half years ago.

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:38.224
We're getting geared up.

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:39.492
We go to see this play.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.227
Blair Underwood
is one of the leads.

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.296
Legendary, most handsome man
in the world,

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:44.531
Blair Underwood.

00:02:44.531 --> 00:02:46.533
And we start,
like, the second act,

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:49.102
and, you know, I know that I'm
going to do this nude scene.

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:50.169
And I'm watching this play.

00:02:50.169 --> 00:02:51.637
And he --
It starts the second act.

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:53.572
He's buttoning the top three
buttons of a button-down shirt.

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:54.974
He's fully dressed.

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:57.310
He's just buttoning the
top three buttons of his shirt.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.478
And the audience goes, "Wow."

00:02:58.478 --> 00:02:59.845
The ladies are
hootin' and hollerin'.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:01.680
Like, shut -- The production
has to wait like

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.049
an extra 10 seconds.

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:04.317
And I was like, "Oh, my God.

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:05.852
What are they
going to do for full-on

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:09.087
seven -- two naked men
out on the stage?

00:03:09.087 --> 00:03:12.058
But we kind of get the
opposite reaction in our play.

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:16.662
It's so intense
and the language is so dense

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:18.998
that as soon as naked people
come out, everybody gets quiet.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:21.000
You can hear a pin drop
or something else drop.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.334
-Right, right.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:25.238
-It gets really quiet
every time we come out there.

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:28.174
So, every now and then,
there's some shrieks or gasps

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:30.009
or people scurrying out.

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:33.145
The light of the exit door
cascades up there.

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:34.413
-And then people in the street
see the nudity,

00:03:34.413 --> 00:03:35.614
and they're like, "Whoa!"

00:03:35.614 --> 00:03:38.318
-It's a whole different
licensing and stuff.

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:39.953
-When was the last --
What was the last

00:03:39.953 --> 00:03:42.555
organized baseball game
you played?

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:44.323
-Oh, I played until high school.
-Okay.

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:46.425
-I played pretty intensely,
traveled.

00:03:46.425 --> 00:03:49.194
It was a really big connective
thread for me and my dad.

00:03:49.194 --> 00:03:51.063
My brother played.
He was even better than me.

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:52.431
Baseball was everything.

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:55.935
I grew up in Chicago, so the
Cubs, going to Cubs games.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:58.905
And so baseball was a huge part
of my life and life lessons,

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:00.974
all those kind of
cliché you know,

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:04.076
metaphors were taught to me.

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:07.913
So it was a big part of
my existence.

00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:10.183
So that's a big part of
my connection to the character.

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:12.084
-So, was your dad a coach?
-He coached us, yeah.

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:13.318
-Was he a good coach?

00:04:13.318 --> 00:04:16.388
How was his -- I had a dad
coached me, as well,

00:04:16.388 --> 00:04:19.191
and it maybe sounds like
it might be a little different.

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:22.061
-Well, so, I kind of
hated it then,

00:04:22.061 --> 00:04:23.329
but you appreciate it later.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:25.965
He was very intense,
and everything was a lesson.

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:27.799
Everything was a life lesson.
Everything was a challenge.

00:04:27.799 --> 00:04:30.169
I would be 6 years old --
Even just getting to practice,

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:31.570
I'd be like,
"Dad, when's practice?"

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.739
He's like, "You don't know?
Find out. Call your coach."

00:04:33.739 --> 00:04:35.674
It's like, "I'm 6."

00:04:35.674 --> 00:04:37.543
"Nope. Call him, find out.

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:39.879
You don't depend on anybody
for any information ever."

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:42.114
"Okay. Let me just finish my
Cap'n Crunch,

00:04:42.114 --> 00:04:44.750
and then I'll go talk to
this adult man

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.586
and figure out my scheduling."

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:47.820
Yeah, everything was
pretty intense.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.689
-My dad dovetailed very nicely,
because he was

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:52.925
the kind of baseball coach --
also very intense.

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:55.128
And then I think someone,
some of the other dads

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:56.261
took him aside and they go,

00:04:56.261 --> 00:04:58.764
"You seem like you
should be an umpire."

00:04:58.764 --> 00:05:00.633
And then my dad was
an umpire for years and years.

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:02.034
-To get him away from
the children.

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:03.402
-They were like,
"There's a place where

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:04.637
it's okay to scream at them."

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:06.539
-Right, right,
right, right, right.

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:07.906
Not while you're handing out
orange slices.

00:05:07.906 --> 00:05:10.109
-Yeah, exactly.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:12.177
-There's a good place where you
can scream after every pitch.

00:05:12.177 --> 00:05:13.279
-Right? Exactly.
-Yeah.

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.747
And, yeah,
he found a very nice home there.

00:05:15.747 --> 00:05:18.184
-That's funny.
-And your parents are teachers?

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.686
-They are. Yeah, they were.
My mom just retired.

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:21.820
-Alright.
-They both were teachers.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.657
Yeah, I was a teacher,

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.259
partly because of them,
certainly.

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:28.895
They didn't start teaching until
I was in college.

00:05:28.895 --> 00:05:30.162
-Okay.

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:31.663
-So they had
many other jobs before then.

00:05:31.663 --> 00:05:33.198
But, yeah, my mom just retired,

00:05:33.198 --> 00:05:35.834
and my dad just finished up a
couple years ago, as well.

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:37.236
-What did you teach?

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:38.971
-I taught high school,
primarily, in Philadelphia,

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:40.039
where I went to college.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:41.240
I went to Temple University.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.576
I primarily taught humanities,
American studies,

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:46.345
African-American studies,
and continental African history.

00:05:46.345 --> 00:05:48.480
-Were you a good teacher?
-I was a damn good teacher.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.583
-Really?
-Yeah, I really was.

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:51.584
I loved it.

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:53.185
It was my
favorite job ever, still.

00:05:53.185 --> 00:05:55.921
I love my job now, but that
was incredibly rewarding.

00:05:55.921 --> 00:05:57.122
And I felt --

00:05:57.122 --> 00:06:02.595
-Did you feel there was a
common skill as far as

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:05.131
a performer, being both
an actor and a teacher?

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:07.600
-I learned it later,
that there is a commonality.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.935
You know, you're hosting
a room full of people,

00:06:09.935 --> 00:06:11.437
a space full of people that
don't necessarily want

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:12.938
to be there.

00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:15.140
You gotta keep their attention.
You got to keep them engaged.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.442
You got to be spontaneous.

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:17.809
You got to be able to
improv and ad-lib

00:06:17.809 --> 00:06:20.846
and keep things alive
and make it interesting

00:06:20.846 --> 00:06:22.915
while also, you know, putting
some sugar in the medicine

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:24.717
or medicine in the sugar,
keeping --

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.686
-In the most -- I think that
age in particular

00:06:27.686 --> 00:06:29.255
is the most intimidating age
of people to be in front of.

00:06:29.255 --> 00:06:30.623
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:33.825
High schoolers will run you
to the ground, yeah, yeah.

00:06:33.825 --> 00:06:36.762
-I'd rather do a show in
a prison than in a high school.

00:06:36.762 --> 00:06:38.631
-High school is terrifying.

00:06:38.631 --> 00:06:39.865
-Yeah.
-It's brutal.

00:06:39.865 --> 00:06:40.933
Because they're also
funny as hell.

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:42.067
They'll rip on you.

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:45.338
I remember this kid
ripped on my boots.

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:46.873
I was poor.
I didn't have any money.

00:06:46.873 --> 00:06:48.174
And I had the same winter --

00:06:48.174 --> 00:06:49.208
You know,
it's Philadelphia winters.

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:50.777
I had the same boots for
like seven years.

00:06:50.777 --> 00:06:52.511
They were all, like, shredded.
-Yeah.

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:53.846
-And this kid --
he was so funny.

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:56.815
He was like --
He looked down at my boots

00:06:56.815 --> 00:06:58.350
and he's like,
"Damn, Mr. Williams.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.353
I didn't know you had a dog."

00:07:01.353 --> 00:07:03.522
And it was brilliant.

00:07:03.522 --> 00:07:06.159
I couldn't even be mad.
It was hilarious.

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:07.960
And my shoes looked like they
had been chewed on by a dog.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.128
It was good.

00:07:09.128 --> 00:07:10.629
So shout-out to you.
I forget his name now.

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:12.298
-Yeah. Well,
if you remembered his name,

00:07:12.298 --> 00:07:13.732
I would hope you'd show up
at his house now

00:07:13.732 --> 00:07:14.767
and show him your nice boots.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:15.968
-Yeah, exactly, exactly.

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:17.369
-Things worked out pretty well
for Mr. Williams.

00:07:17.369 --> 00:07:20.439
-Who's laughing now?

00:07:20.439 --> 00:07:23.041
-Your kids are 6 and 8.
-Yeah.

00:07:23.041 --> 00:07:24.143
-And they've come out
to New York

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:25.644
since you started doing this.

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:28.514
I would imagine the play you're
currently in is not for them.

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:31.117
-It's not for them, but I did --
You know, you work around it.

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:33.986
It's their first time really
exploring New York City,

00:07:33.986 --> 00:07:35.821
so they're watching people.

00:07:35.821 --> 00:07:37.456
Watching your kids discover
New York,

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:39.959
and the awesome power of it
is spectacular.

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:41.993
So, you take them around.
We saw "Lion King."

00:07:41.993 --> 00:07:44.530
We saw "Harry Potter,"
which we're reading together.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.766
And I took them to
the theater, our theater,

00:07:47.766 --> 00:07:49.268
just to see our vocal warm-ups.

00:07:49.268 --> 00:07:50.936
Like, but they can't
see the play,

00:07:50.936 --> 00:07:53.039
but they can see the
really silly vocal warm-ups.

00:07:53.039 --> 00:07:54.539
And for those of you
that don't have

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:56.308
theater or musical-theater
people in your life,

00:07:56.308 --> 00:07:57.309
we look ridiculous.

00:07:57.309 --> 00:07:58.777
-Yeah.
-It's hilarious.

00:07:58.777 --> 00:08:01.780
Yeah, 15, you know,
grown men onstage

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:04.583
jumping around like monkeys
and making all types of

00:08:04.583 --> 00:08:06.819
animal sounds
and doing stretching

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.955
and banging
on their chest and...

00:08:10.489 --> 00:08:12.724
It looks ridiculous.
So they thought that was great.

00:08:12.724 --> 00:08:14.159
They thought,
"Daddy's an idiot."

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:16.128
And it's really fun.
They watched that.

00:08:16.128 --> 00:08:17.296
And then I sent them on
to go do something.

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:20.032
-Okay. How is --
Our 6-year-old is our oldest,

00:08:20.032 --> 00:08:21.700
and he just --
He went to "Lion King."

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:22.835
That was his first show.

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:24.803
We just went
a couple of months ago.

00:08:24.803 --> 00:08:26.071
Did they love it?
-They loved it.

00:08:26.071 --> 00:08:29.508
-Yeah.
-They were just in awe.

00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:33.011
And by the very end, they were
kind of getting a little --

00:08:33.011 --> 00:08:34.113
It's long.

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:36.148
-Yeah.
-But it's spectacular.

00:08:36.148 --> 00:08:37.750
It's really awesome,
in the true sense of the word.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.619
-An interesting thing
happened to my 6-year-old,

00:08:39.619 --> 00:08:41.587
who has a very early bedtime,
which is,

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:43.856
he went to "Lion King"
and then, like,

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:47.126
drove through Times Square
at night to go home, you know,

00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:50.062
with with my wife in the Uber.

00:08:50.062 --> 00:08:51.897
And, so, he got home,
and by the time he got home,

00:08:51.897 --> 00:08:54.500
like, Times Square
was cooler than "Lion King."

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:58.837
He's like, "You cannot believe
what's going on in this city."

00:08:58.837 --> 00:09:01.707
-Right. It's the most
recent stimulus is the best.

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:03.275
-I'm like, "Tell me
more about 'The Lion King.'"

00:09:03.275 --> 00:09:05.077
He's like,
"Dad, there are screens

00:09:05.077 --> 00:09:06.379
that are just
changing constantly."

00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:08.514
-"Have you heard of Samsung?"
-Yeah.

00:09:08.514 --> 00:09:10.849
"I need to tell you
about Samsung.

00:09:10.849 --> 00:09:12.785
Their product line..."

00:09:13.818 --> 00:09:15.354
-Their new rollout...

00:09:15.354 --> 00:09:17.489
-Well, congratulations on
a Broadway debut.

00:09:17.489 --> 00:09:19.258
It's really nice having
you here in New York City,

00:09:19.258 --> 00:09:20.860
and thanks for
being with us tonight.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.127
-Please come out
and check out the show.

00:09:22.127 --> 00:09:23.295
We're having a great time.

00:09:23.295 --> 00:09:24.730
-Alright, you guys.
That's Jesse Williams.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.165
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:26.165 --> 00:09:28.367
-"Take Me Out" is currently
playing at the Hayes Theater.

